Questions_Grade_8:
Q: You are creating a face filter project in Scratch that changes filters (costumes) based on the amount of motion detected by the camera. You want the filters to change smoothly and not too quickly. Which of the following code structures would best accomplish this, assuming that video sensing is set up and functioning?

A. forever { next costume }
B. when green flag clicked { forever { next costume } }
C. forever { wait until <video motion on sprite > 50> next costume }
D. forever { wait until <video motion on sprite > 50> next costume wait (0.5) seconds }

Correct: D
Concept: Sequencing within a Loop
Explanation: Option D provides the most control by combining motion detection using 'wait until <video motion on sprite > 50>', costume changes with 'next costume', and a brief pause using 'wait (0.5) seconds'. This ensures that the filter only changes when there is sufficient motion and also prevents it from changing too rapidly. Option A would change costumes constantly, Option B is similar but starts when the green flag is clicked which is required, and Option C would change costumes immediately after motion is detected without a delay.

Questions_Grade_7:
Q: A program draws a different shape each time the mouse is clicked. The program uses a variable called 'shapeCount' to keep track of how many times the mouse has been clicked. After every 3 clicks, the program resets 'shapeCount' back to 1. Which of the following code snippets would accomplish this?

A. if (shapeCount > 3) { shapeCount = 1; }
B. if (shapeCount = 3) { shapeCount = 1; }
C. if (shapeCount < 3) { shapeCount = 1; }
D. if (shapeCount != 3) { shapeCount = 1; }

Correct: A
Concept: Creating a Cyclical Sequence
Explanation: The correct code `if (shapeCount > 3) { shapeCount = 1; }` resets the counter only when it exceeds 3.  Option B uses the assignment operator (=) instead of the comparison operator (== or === in some languages), and also only triggers *when* it is 3, so will never increase past 1. Option C resets the count if it's *less* than 3, so it will always be 1. Option D resets if it's *not* 3, so it will only allow 3 to appear once.
